t west africa that's  triggering these recent schools  well i think it's always important to um  you know to remind that  the countries of the continent and even  within a region like west africa have  their own political trajectory and so  when you comment on the coup for example  in mali uh it is not the same thing as  commenting the circumstances of a coping  guinea so i think we have to be cautious  about taking these groups as something  that can be generalized as a trend in in  west africa there is certainly a trend  of democratic regression in the in the  region but i would not necessarily take  the case of mali for example as an  example of that because mali is facing a  security crisis and the coup was not a  surprise in the country like that  okay but but then still on that what is  giving rise to cruz in the region than  you think  i think the main issue is uh the way the  countries are governed i think that when  we talk about democracy uh everywhere in  the world uh we we have to recognize  that it is something that is built and  that is something that so which is  linked to uh with the capacity of a  political system to produce security to  produce economic and social benefit to  the people so when you do have a  democratic system with regular elections  but we you who don't have a sense that  the state is providing a security is  providing um jobs for the youths it's  providing economic and social  development for the people basically you  have a failure in uh you know the belief  in the democratic systems and i think  that used to give a pretext for the  military uh to enter in the political  scene and to say that we can bring  especially security back  we have best place to to bring uh what  the people need and i think that is a  that's a major issue  so regional bodies like ecoass and the  eu don't seem to be able to force cool  leaders to return to democratic ways why  is that eu and ecowas have developed a  lot of protocols on democracy and good 